VeeR-EL2's testbench declares its ECC error injection mode with no initial
value, so a 4-state simulation starts it as x (IEEE 1800-2023 6.8, Table 6-7).
The DCCM injection block then never takes its "injection off" branch, since
12.4 does not take a branch whose condition is x, and every DCCM write gets a
bit flipped. The program still passes, but the core corrects each flip and
runs more cycles than the same program on Verilator, where the mode starts at
0. Start it at 0, as upstream VeeR-EL2 has reset it since
chipsalliance/Cores-VeeR-EL2@21c5e9b; nothing changes on Verilator.

Sent to the suite as https://github.com/verilator/rtlmeter/pull/63

diff --git a/designs/VeeR-EL2/src/tb_top.sv b/designs/VeeR-EL2/src/tb_top.sv
index 6cd3df7..6b7ed7f 100644
--- a/designs/VeeR-EL2/src/tb_top.sv
+++ b/designs/VeeR-EL2/src/tb_top.sv
@@ -522,7 +522,8 @@ module tb_top
     logic [pt.DCCM_NUM_BANKS-1:0][pt.DCCM_FDATA_WIDTH-1:0] dccm_wr_fdata_bank;
     logic [pt.DCCM_NUM_BANKS-1:0][pt.DCCM_FDATA_WIDTH-1:0] dccm_bank_fdout;

-    tb_top_pkg::veer_sram_error_injection_mode_t error_injection_mode;
+    // Upstream resets this to 0 since chipsalliance/Cores-VeeR-EL2@21c5e9b.
+    tb_top_pkg::veer_sram_error_injection_mode_t error_injection_mode = '0;

